/* ---- task #252: File Nav, LEFT of the ruler. Class names are deliberately the ones the real
   component already ships in webapp/views/sidebar.ts + views/timeline-file-tree.ts, because the
   task is to REUSE that component, not to write a second tree. */

/* One column, two pickers, a horizontal grip between them (user, 2026-07-27). `--files-share` is
   the split as a percentage of the column, so the two panes always add up to the column's height
   and neither can be dragged out of existence. */

/* The pane's title row and its search box stay put while the TREE scrolls under them: a header
   that scrolls away takes the "Show Only Selected" toggle with it. Negative margins pull it out
   to the pane's edges so nothing shows through beside it. */

/* task #292: the JSONL picker is a second SECTION of the File Nav pane, not a third column — it
   filters the timeline the same way the folder tree does, so it belongs beside it and inherits the
   pane's width, its scroll and its resize grip. */

/* task #303: the highlight-then-fade. --c-echo, deliberately NOT the --c-script a SELECTED session
   wears — this mark does not filter anything, and two marks that look alike would say it does.
   The duration comes from FLASH_MS via the inline --flash-ms, so there is one number to change. */

/* Sticky gutter: the ruler stays put while the canvas scrolls left. --rail-x is derived from the
   widest label — task #276 put seconds and hundredths in ("MM-DD HH:MM:SS.hh") and task #275
   appended " (n)", so 23 monospace characters at 10 px x 0.6 em = ~140 px, plus 10 px clearance. */

/* Zoom is the BUBBLES' scale, not the page's: `zoom` (never `transform: scale()`) participates in
   layout, so zooming out genuinely shrinks the scroll area and more bubbles fit on screen, while
   the gutter outside it keeps its own font size at every zoom (user, 2026-07-26). */

/* Half-transparent so the JSONL wash behind it reads THROUGH the bubble (user, 2026-07-27) —
   otherwise the one place the range matters most, over the files it touched, is the one place
   it cannot be seen. */

/* task #245/#270/#280: the label is the BASENAME with the full path on `data-path`, ellipsised to
   the bubble at rest so it cannot overprint its neighbours. #270's wrapped two-line name was
   REVERTED on user feedback; truncate-plus-reveal is the settled treatment. */

/* task #292: where a selected JSONL starts and ends. The filter keeps each touched file's WHOLE
   history, so the band is the only thing saying which part of that history the session actually
   wrote — it rides the same axis-times-zoom conversion the ticks and leaders use. */

/* `:not(.found)` — a SELECTED node already wears its [ ] brackets, and the hover ring is a second
   mark in the same colour landing in the same few px (user, 2026-07-27). The selection is the
   stronger statement, so it wins and the hover says nothing extra. */

/* task #259: two events of ONE file at ONE instant (a commit and the working tree touched in the
   same second) get their own row slots — the rounded rectangle is what says they are simultaneous
   rather than merely adjacent. Drawn before the nodes so it stays behind them. */

/* Each row is placed at its OWN instant (see addBucket), so the list is a positioning context
   tall enough to hold the last one. `translateY(-50%)` centres a row on its instant the way a
   `.node` is centred on its own — otherwise a row hangs half a line below its ruler timestamp. */

/* ---- task #246: minimap, pinned to the LOWER-LEFT of the timeline pane. It lives in .stagewrap,
   NOT in the scrolling .timelines, so it does not scroll away.
   task #269: inset past the sticky ruler — at left: 12px it covered the gutter's timestamps. */

/* task #294: the drawer's code colours. The real page reuses webapp/highlight.ts, whose renderCodeInto
   already serves the revision view — these class names are that renderer's output shape, so the port
   is a swap of the tokeniser and not of the stylesheet. */

/* ---- tasks #323/#324: the drawer's control strips.
   Row 1 carries identity and navigation, row 2 the diff tools — and row 2 exists ONLY for a pair,
   so a single-node view spends none of the drawer's height on buttons it cannot use. */

/* task #305: the pair's marks on the lane. They REPLACE the [ ] brackets rather than joining them
   — one selection language at a time — so they are rings, and the two ends differ in colour
   because "which of these two is the base" is the question the header answers in words. */
